    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Clarify the requirement.\n\n**How should token rotation work?** should separate identity, session state, and authorization. Keep sensitive credentials protected, validate the flow at trusted boundaries, and model expiry/recovery explicitly.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nStart with users, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, scale assumptions, state/data flow, and failure modes. Choose the smallest architecture that satisfies the constraints.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision, state transition, lookup, or contract so you can explain the reasoning line by line in a system-design interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface Session {\n  userId: string;\n  expiresAt: number;\n}\n\nfunction isSessionValid(\n  session: Session,\n  now: number,\n): boolean {\n  return (\n    session.userId.length > 0 &&\n    session.expiresAt > now\n  );\n}\n\nconst valid = isSessionValid(\n  { userId: \"u1\", expiresAt: 2000 },\n  1000,\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they communicate the same architecture clearly and do not hide an important trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst session = {\n  userId: \"u1\",\n  expiresAt: 2000,\n};\n\nconst valid =\n  Boolean(session.userId) &&\n  session.expiresAt > 1000;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **How should token rotation work?** with explicit session/credential lifecycle rules, secure defaults, failure states, and trusted server-side enforcement for identity and permissions.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant read/write/render/network cost, the expected growth path, and where caching, partitioning, batching, pagination, or independent deployment would become useful.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty data, partial failure, retries, stale state, unauthorized access, slow networks, large datasets, migration constraints, and rollback where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, the source of truth, the failure/recovery path, the observability signal, and why this design is simpler or safer than the alternatives.",
